WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:03.840
We wanted to let you know here&nbsp;
at the top of today’s episode&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:07.200
that Journey to the Microcosmos is now on TikTok.

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:10.716
You can think of our TikToks like the&nbsp;
fun size candy bar of Microcosmos.

00:00:10.716 --> 00:00:14.539
You will still find your full length,&nbsp;
full size Microcosmos here on YouTube,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.539 --> 00:00:17.516
but if you’re looking for a&nbsp;
quick little Microcosmos snack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.516 --> 00:00:21.738
you can check us out on TikTok, where&nbsp;
we are @journeytothemicrocosmos.

00:00:43.136 --> 00:00:45.193
Given the many&nbsp;fascinating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.193 --> 00:00:50.332
single-celled organisms that we have seen&nbsp;
on our journey through the microcosmos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.332 --> 00:00:57.360
it would be easy to mistake this for yet another&nbsp;
fascinating, spiky ciliate—perhaps something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:04.383
related to a suctorian, but with an unusually&nbsp;
giant organelle pulsating within its boundaries.

00:01:04.383 --> 00:01:07.440
But this is not a single-celled organism.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:12.890
This… is an egg, which is&nbsp;
also a thrilling sight for us.

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:16.395
Eggs are incredible.

00:01:16.395 --> 00:01:19.320
Whether they are kept within&nbsp;
the confines of their mother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:27.487
or—as with this one—released into the&nbsp;
world, the egg is all about what comes next.

00:01:27.487 --> 00:01:34.740
Within its confines are cells that divide&nbsp;
and differentiate, gathering in numbers while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:41.515
creating their own individual identities that will&nbsp;
become organs and tissue in the animal to come.

00:01:41.515 --> 00:01:48.937
And you can see the animal moving around in its&nbsp;
egg, restless and perhaps ready to hatch.

00:01:48.937 --> 00:01:54.400
And when it does, it will take on the&nbsp;
cumulative identity of all those cells,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:58.303
in this case, an animal that we call 
the Gastrotrich.

00:02:06.468 --> 00:02:08.880
The Gastrotrich has long been a personal favorite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:13.459
microbe of several members of the&nbsp;
Journey to the Microcosmos crew.

00:02:13.459 --> 00:02:17.400
They were one of the first microbes&nbsp;
we dedicated an entire episode to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:23.550
and many of us came away charmed by&nbsp;
their hairy, dragon-shaped bodies.

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:27.147
But while we were able to see a lot&nbsp;
with the microscopes we had at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.147 --> 00:02:29.880
James—our master of microscopes—has made some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:34.571
significant upgrades since then (thanks&nbsp;
in part to the support of many of you).

00:02:34.571 --> 00:02:40.232
And this means that we are now able to see&nbsp;
gastrotrichs in a whole new light.

00:02:40.232 --> 00:02:44.760
So while before, we could see the hair&nbsp;
traveling down the length of their bodies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:49.308
and even make out the organs within them,&nbsp;
now it’s like you can touch them.

00:02:49.308 --> 00:02:51.600
Like if you run your finger along them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:58.363
you will feel the prickle of scales and the&nbsp;
feathery brush of hairs against your skin.

00:02:58.363 --> 00:03:03.117
And to be honest, that was our main&nbsp;
reason for wanting to revisit them today.

00:03:03.117 --> 00:03:07.885
But while we are here, perhaps we should revisit&nbsp;
the gastrotrich more generally.

00:03:07.885 --> 00:03:10.614
If you haven’t watched it before&nbsp;
(or just want a refresher),&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.614 --> 00:03:12.767
feel free to take a moment now&nbsp;
to rewatch it if you’d like.

00:03:12.767 --> 00:03:14.701
We will still be here when you’re done.

00:03:14.701 --> 00:03:18.480
But if you’d rather stick around, the main&nbsp;
thing you need to know is that we titled that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:23.899
episode “Four Day Old Grandmothers,”&nbsp;
because for some gastrotrich species,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.899 --> 00:03:28.315
reproduction operates on a very rushed timeline.

00:03:28.315 --> 00:03:31.062
Those species are members of the order Chaetonida,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.062 --> 00:03:36.512
which sets them in contrast to the other&nbsp;
order of Gastrotrichs, the Macrodasyida.

00:03:36.512 --> 00:03:40.860
While almost all of gastrotrichs across&nbsp;
both orders can reproduce sexually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:45.000
the Chaetonids have an additional&nbsp;
approach: parthenogenesis.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:50.049
This means that they are able to form&nbsp;
an embryo from an unfertilized egg.

00:03:50.049 --> 00:03:55.080
The reason we’re revisiting the specifics of&nbsp;
gastrotrich reproduction is because it might be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.080 --> 00:04:01.464
related to another fundamental difference between&nbsp;
Chaetonids and Macrodasyoids: their habitats.

00:04:01.464 --> 00:04:08.229
Chaetonids are largely found in freshwater, while&nbsp;
Macrodasyoids are mostly found in marine waters.

00:04:08.229 --> 00:04:12.000
Scientists have long wondered&nbsp;
just how Gastrotrichs found

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.775
themselves in these different environments.

00:04:14.775 --> 00:04:20.040
It’s generally accepted that the order&nbsp;
Macrodasyida is older than the order Chaetonida.

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:25.440
That suggests that Gastrotrichs began&nbsp;
as an order of marine animals, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:31.681
then went through some evolutionary pathway that&nbsp;
transitioned some of them into freshwater life.

00:04:31.681 --> 00:04:36.000
There are many questions that&nbsp;
we could ask about that transition.

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:38.460
Where did the gastrotrichs’ journey take them?

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:42.360
Did this evolution happen&nbsp;
several times, or just once?

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:46.200
Did changes on a geologic&nbsp;
scale—like changes in sea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:50.155
level—shape these long-lasting&nbsp;
changes within their bodies?

00:04:50.155 --> 00:04:52.920
Is the journey of Chaetonids&nbsp;
into freshwater complete,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:56.700
or did any ever venture their&nbsp;
way back to marine environments?

00:04:57.213 --> 00:05:02.940
Well when scientists used the tools of molecular&nbsp;
phylogeny, they found evidence that the shift&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:09.000
from marine to freshwater life happened just&nbsp;
once, and that it happened fairly quickly.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:13.927
And it may be that parthenogenesis&nbsp;
made that change possible.

00:05:13.927 --> 00:05:19.549
If some of those gastrotrichs in transition&nbsp;
had been able to reproduce via parthenogenesis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.549 --> 00:05:24.146
they may have had a significant advantage&nbsp;
as they settled in a new environment.

00:05:24.146 --> 00:05:26.487
Without having to go through&nbsp;
the trouble of fertilization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.487 --> 00:05:32.460
they could have quickly expanded their population,&nbsp;
gaining a foothold that allowed them to survive.

00:05:33.062 --> 00:05:37.200
Now we don’t know if parthenogenesis&nbsp;
did directly drive the chaetonids’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:43.008
ability to transition into freshwater, but&nbsp;
there is evolutionary precedent for it.

00:05:43.008 --> 00:05:49.172
Mud snails shifted to parthenogenetic reproduction&nbsp;
in their own marine-to-freshwater transition.

00:05:49.172 --> 00:05:52.380
But parthenogenesis is just one of the traits that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:56.455
might have allowed these species&nbsp;
to spread into less salty waters.

00:05:56.455 --> 00:06:00.960
They would have required other adaptations,&nbsp;
changes that would allow them to tolerate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:05.629
the shifting landscape in the&nbsp;
ecology and geology around them.

00:06:05.629 --> 00:06:08.460
Scientists have also found that there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:13.321
some marine gastrotrichs among the&nbsp;
otherwise freshwater chaetonids.

00:06:13.321 --> 00:06:17.460
It might seem like this relationship&nbsp;
means those marine gastrotrichs are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:22.540
remnants of the ancient gastrotrichs&nbsp;
who set the stage for freshwater life.

00:06:22.540 --> 00:06:27.780
But that’s not what the genetics say.&nbsp;
Instead, it appears that these marine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:34.000
gastrotrichs are the result of chaetonids who&nbsp;
decided that freshwater life wasn’t for them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:38.480
who returned to the saltier&nbsp;
environments of their ancestors.

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:40.746
What drove those gastrotrichs back?

00:06:40.746 --> 00:06:44.781
Well aside from the fact that there 
was an evolutionary space for them.

00:06:44.781 --> 00:06:46.052
We don’t know.

00:06:46.052 --> 00:06:51.905
Animals have been navigating new environments&nbsp;
and finding ways to adapt for eons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.905 --> 00:07:00.740
each organism always laying the foundation for&nbsp;
new ways of living, building a biology so complex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.740 --> 00:07:07.011
that even the tiniest bits of it are worth of&nbsp;
the sort of intense, unrelenting investigation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.011 --> 00:07:10.096
that humans are so very, very good at.

00:07:16.939 --> 00:07:22.687
Thank you for coming on this journey with us as&nbsp;
we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:07:22.687 --> 00:07:26.400
The people on the screen right&nbsp;
now, those are our Patreon patrons.

